00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:10.770
So Haben, thank you so
much for joining us.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:11.770
HABEN GIRMA: Absolutely.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.065
Thanks for having
me here, Melanie.

00:04:14.065 --> 00:04:15.700
And hi, Mark.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.600
MARK MANDEL: Hi.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.510
Before we get started today
and talk about accessibility

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.561
in tech, can you tell us
about you and what do you do?

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:24.060
HABEN GIRMA: My
name is Haben Girma.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.500
And I'm a disability
rights advocate.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.980
I teach individuals
and organizations

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.880
how to design with
access in mind.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.840
One of the reasons I do
this is because I myself

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.420
have a disability.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.450
I'm deaf-blind.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.810
I have limited
vision and hearing.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.040
There are all these
alternative techniques

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.290
for accessing information.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.540
And I've been successful,
because I've found

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:48.910
lots of alternative techniques.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:53.510
And I've found communities that
value alternative techniques.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.720
For example, during
this podcast,

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.500
as Melanie or Mark
speak, their words

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.390
are being transcribed and
output into digital braille.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:07.090
And I'm feeling their
words in digital braille

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.250
and then responding by voice.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.410
And this technology
is an example

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.450
of an alternative technique.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:16.080
And people with disabilities
have lots of different tools

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.890
and solutions that we use.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.840
MELANIE WARRICK: What
drove you to become

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.430
a speaker and a driven
advocate on this topic?

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.830
HABEN GIRMA: Disability
has the potential

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.450
to be an opportunity
for innovation.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.900
And a lot of people
don't know that.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.660
And I'm really passionate
about teaching organizations,

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.520
especially those who focus
on designing technology,

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.270
to think of disability as an
opportunity for innovation.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.090
For example, email--

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.930
Vint Cerf, one of the
founders of the internet,

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.270
helped develop one of the
earliest email protocols.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.340
And this was back
before the internet

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.870
existed as we know it today,
back before deaf individuals

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.470
had a good tool for
communicating long distance,

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:05.290
because we didn't have good
access to the telephone.

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:08.920
Email was a way to
communicate long distance.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.140
And you could
understand a message

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.410
without struggling to hear.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.120
Nowadays, lots of
people use email--

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.640
hearing people, deaf people.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.000
And a lot of the challenges
people with disabilities

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.320
face from communication
to accessing print, when

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:31.060
you look for a solution, you'll
end up finding a solution that

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.760
not only helps people
with disabilities,

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.090
but helps the greater population
and becomes the next big thing.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.270
So there are lots
of hidden stories

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:42.550
out there that people
don't know about.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:45.380
And I'm excited to teach
people these stories

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:48.220
so that more
designers, developers,

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:50.980
people who work in the
field of technology

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:53.535
can start investing
in accessibility.

00:06:53.535 --> 00:06:54.910
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's so great.

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:57.790
And we actually have a
podcast on Vint Cerf,

00:06:57.790 --> 00:06:59.642
so it's funny that
you mention him.

00:06:59.642 --> 00:07:00.600
HABEN GIRMA: Excellent.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:02.530
I didn't know you did.

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:03.670
That's perfect.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.650
I know he's here
at Google, right?

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.340
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, he is.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.280
MARK MANDEL: So you talk a
lot about tech accessibility.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:11.920
How do you define that?

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.710
HABEN GIRMA: Accessibility
means lots of different things.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:19.450
The goal is to reach as
many people as possible,

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:23.500
to build one product,
one app, one website

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:26.650
that the maximum number
of people can use.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.175
And there are different
elements to accessibility.

00:07:29.175 --> 00:07:33.070
There is visual accessibility,
making sure blind people can

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:34.420
access the service.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:37.210
The Web Content
Accessibility Guidelines

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.390
helps teach how to make
websites accessible.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:44.470
For apps, there's Android
Accessibility Guidelines.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:48.740
And for iOS, there's Apple
Accessibility Guidelines.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.370
So if you're making
something, make

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.280
sure you follow the
accessibility guidelines

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.790
so that it's accessible
to the blind community.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:00.010
For the deaf community,
the big thing

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:03.730
is captions, making sure
videos have captions

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:07.150
so that deaf individuals
can access the audio content

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:11.290
or for podcasts,
including transcripts

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.770
so that people can access
the content of the podcasts.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:16.570
Those are some examples.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.870
But it's not the limit.

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:21.220
There are lots of
accessibility features

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:25.360
that are waiting to be built.
And if we keep innovating,

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.910
we'll come up with new
solutions that will make

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.010
even more things accessible.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:33.460
Self-driving cars-- a lot
of people with disabilities

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.250
are excited about
self-driving cars.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:40.020
We're also worried that we
won't be able to use them.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.780
So we're hopeful that they'll
be designed with access in mind

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:47.590
and future technologies will
be designed with access in mind

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:49.750
so lots of people can use them.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:53.140
MELANIE WARRICK: When I saw
you speak at Black in AI,

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.870
what resonated the most
with me was your point

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:59.830
about how it should be one
technology for everyone,

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:01.480
not multiple.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:04.180
And that also goes to what
you're saying right now.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.970
Did you want to talk through
a little bit about that point?

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:09.280
HABEN GIRMA: That's
a great point.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.320
Some people, when they
think about accessibility,

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:16.600
they think of a separate special
service, so a separate app

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.910
for people with disabilities,
a separate website

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:20.820
for people with disabilities.

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.690
Separate is never equal.

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:25.480
You might start out
with good intentions

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:30.170
and intend for the two services
to be equal, but down the line,

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.640
the one for people
with disabilities

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.010
won't get updated as often.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.150
It won't have all the features.

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:39.910
And it just becomes inferior.

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:41.770
Separate is never equal.

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:46.510
Instead what we want is one
service, one app designed

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:47.950
for everyone to use it.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.260
It becomes easier for everyone.

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:56.010
And some designers immediately
think of design problems,

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:59.410
because they're not used to
thinking about accessibility.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:02.510
Constraints often
spark innovation

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.070
and spark new ways
of doing things.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.070
If you're not sure how
to do something, ask.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:10.380
Google has an
accessibility team,

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:13.440
so folks here can ask
people about accessibility.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.420
Reach out to the
accessibility team

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.240
or reach out to
disability organizations.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.180
There are lots of
organizations in the Bay Area.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.770
There's a San
Francisco lighthouse

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:25.920
for the blind, nationally
National Federation

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.970
of the Blind, or for deaf
services, National Association

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:31.110
of the Deaf.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:32.530
Ask questions.

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.470
Reach out to the communities.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:35.517
Don't make assumptions.

00:10:35.517 --> 00:10:37.350
MARK MANDEL: What
challenges do you normally

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.420
see from companies
who are potentially

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:41.070
looking to become
more accessible

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.672
but haven't quite got there yet?

00:10:42.672 --> 00:10:48.190
HABEN GIRMA: A challenge
people often cite is resources.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:53.250
And people often say that
accessibility is too expensive.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:58.057
People who say that are
usually jumping to conclusions

00:10:58.057 --> 00:11:01.970
and making assumptions
without doing the research.

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.920
I will say that it does
take more resources

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:08.350
to try to make something
accessible later

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.050
in the stage of development
rather than thinking

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.720
about accessibility
from the start.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:18.520
For example, if somebody
builds a skyscraper and then

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:21.320
after they finish
building it realize, wait,

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:23.740
we forgot elevators,
and then they

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:25.630
have to tear down
part of the building

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:29.410
to add in elevators,
that is more costly

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:32.290
and time consuming
compared to building

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:34.390
an elevator from the start.

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:35.980
Same concept.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:38.590
It's easier to make
something sensible

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:41.970
if you plan for
accessibility from the start

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:44.440
as you're designing
the product or service.

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.830
And if people have more
questions, you can ask.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:50.590
Lots of organizations and
lots of free resources

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.335
online for designing accessibly.

00:11:52.335 --> 00:11:54.460
MELANIE WARRICK: What are
some of the subtle biases

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:58.110
that you've seen in the work and
the research that you've done?

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:02.220
HABEN GIRMA: One bias is the
assumption that the disability

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:07.020
market is very small and
it's a charitable thing

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.330
to design for accessibility.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.070
It's something to
do for charity.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.880
And people don't realize
that the disability

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.932
market is very large.

00:12:16.932 --> 00:12:19.764
There are 1.3 billion
people with disabilities

00:12:19.764 --> 00:12:23.810
around the world, in the
US 57 million Americans

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.380
with disabilities.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:27.240
That's a large market.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:29.960
So if you make your
services accessible,

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:32.960
you get to tap into
that large market, which

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:37.050
means more revenue, more
business opportunities.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:40.820
So it's good business to
invest in accessibility.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.070
Stop thinking about
it as charity.

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:44.690
It's not charity.

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:46.550
It's a great
business opportunity.

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:48.890
MARK MANDEL: Have you
got any great examples

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:51.170
where you've seen
accessibility done really well?

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:55.220
HABEN GIRMA: One classic
example, the city of Berkeley.

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:58.670
Once upon a time,
the city of Berkeley

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:01.580
did not have
accessible sidewalks.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.750
And advocates
complained and advocated

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:08.390
to have full access
to the sidewalks.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:13.670
And Berkeley put in curb
cuts so that wheelchair users

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:17.900
can go on and off the
sidewalk and travel freely

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:19.390
throughout Berkeley.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:21.590
Later, parents
with strollers took

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:22.892
advantage of the curb cuts.

00:13:22.892 --> 00:13:24.350
And they found that
they could push

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:29.330
their toddlers in strollers
on and off very easily.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:34.620
Workers pushing things on
wheels, travelers with luggage,

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:37.070
kids with roller blades--

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:39.440
lots of people
ended up benefiting

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.090
from these curb cuts.

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:46.310
And this concept of a
disability accessibility feature

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:49.130
benefiting the entire
community is now

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.510
called the curb cut effect.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:54.530
And you see it in lots
of different areas.

00:13:54.530 --> 00:13:56.990
Lots of people use elevators.

00:13:56.990 --> 00:13:59.030
And it benefits lots of people.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:03.260
Captions benefit deaf
people, but captions also

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:05.760
benefit hearing people as well.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:09.470
Sometimes hearing people
are in noisy settings.

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:13.370
And they can't quite hear
what's going on on their video,

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:16.460
so they'll turn on captions
so they can fully understand

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:19.994
what's going on in the video,
or maybe they don't want to--

00:14:19.994 --> 00:14:21.410
maybe they don't
want other people

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:23.090
to know they're
watching a video.

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:24.830
And they want to be sneaky.

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:28.460
So they'll turn off the
audio and turn on captions.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:31.220
A marketing study
by Facebook found

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:36.700
that adding captions to
videos increases view time

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:39.350
on average of about 12%.

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:43.380
So lots of benefits
to including captions.

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.020
Curb cut effect.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:47.065
You see it in lots
of different places.

00:14:47.065 --> 00:14:48.440
MELANIE WARRICK:
I love captions.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:49.220
I use them.

00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:51.020
They're very helpful
for me, actually.

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:52.220
HABEN GIRMA: Exactly.

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:53.340
What about you, Mark?

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:54.495
Do you use captions?

00:14:54.495 --> 00:14:55.370
MARK MANDEL: I don't.

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:57.027
My wife definitely does, though.

00:14:57.027 --> 00:14:58.610
MELANIE WARRICK:
They're very helpful.

00:14:58.610 --> 00:14:59.340
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:01.923
MELANIE WARRICK: I know you gave
us some great recommendations

00:15:01.923 --> 00:15:02.570
on resources.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:04.070
Are there any
additional resources

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.320
that you would recommend
for developers and designers

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:08.990
to improve accessibility design?

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:10.520
HABEN GIRMA: I want
to repeat again

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.980
that there are Android
accessibility guidelines

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.030
for developers.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.640
Tap into those and
look into that.

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:20.510
And for websites, Web Content
Accessibility Guidelines.

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:24.680
If you're still not sure, there
are lots of online resources.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:26.570
You can check in with
the San Francisco

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:28.400
Lighthouse for the Blind.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:30.350
You can check in with
the National Federation

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:31.470
of the Blind.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.310
And there are also
accessibility consultants

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:35.760
you can connect with.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:37.880
You're welcome to
reach out to me.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.370
Again, my name is Haben Girma.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:43.416
And my website is
HabenGirma.com.

00:15:43.416 --> 00:15:44.540
MELANIE WARRICK: Thank you.

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:47.180
Haben, one thing I want to
leave on or at least one

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:50.720
of the last things I want to ask
is, you did this wonderful talk

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:52.010
earlier today.

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:54.080
Can you share with
our audience a few

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:57.520
of the key takeaways that you
shared with the audience here?

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:00.730
HABEN GIRMA: The key takeaway
from my talk this morning

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:05.160
is that all the barriers that
exist are created by people.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:06.770
They're arbitrary.

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:08.690
They're not necessary at all.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.960
Disability, for example,
is never a barrier.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:15.480
The barriers created
are created by people.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:21.080
And one example I gave is
how Harvard once upon a time

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.900
used to deny access to women.

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:27.500
Helen Keller, when she was
looking for colleges to attend,

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:29.220
couldn't attend Harvard.

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:32.690
Back then, Harvard
only valued men.

00:16:32.690 --> 00:16:35.810
And Harvard wouldn't
admit women.

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:38.630
Over time, Harvard changed.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:41.030
Society changed.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:43.820
And now Harvard has
opened its doors

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:47.900
to women, people of color,
and people with disabilities.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.300
So the barriers that
exist are created

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.530
by society, not disability.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:56.510
And as developers,
you have the power

00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:00.560
to create tools and
solutions that help people

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:02.670
rather than creating barriers.

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.670
MARK MANDEL: So we're at
a technology conference

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:05.510
here at Next.

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:09.470
Is there any particular
technological advances

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:12.565
that you've seen that you
think are going to break down

00:17:12.565 --> 00:17:13.940
barriers in the
future for people

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:15.589
with different abilities?

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:18.500
HABEN GIRMA: Self-driving
cars have the potential

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:20.869
to break down
barriers if they're

00:17:20.869 --> 00:17:23.150
designed to be accessible.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:27.890
Will wheelchair users be able
to get in and out of these cars

00:17:27.890 --> 00:17:29.470
independently?

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:33.651
Will blind people be able to
drive these cars independently?

00:17:33.651 --> 00:17:36.810
How about someone
with arthritis?

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:40.260
So it really depends on
how they're designed.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:41.340
We're excited.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:43.110
But we're also worried.

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:45.827
So please don't disappoint us.

00:17:45.827 --> 00:17:47.910
MELANIE WARRICK: I think
that's a wonderful point.

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.289
Haben, thank you so
much for taking the time

00:17:50.289 --> 00:17:51.330
to come and chat with us.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:53.160
We really appreciate it.

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:54.940
MARK MANDEL: Yes,
thank you so much.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:56.930
HABEN GIRMA: You're
very welcome.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:58.159
Thanks, Melanie and Mark.

00:17:58.159 --> 00:17:59.700
MELANIE WARRICK:
And thank you again,

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:01.170
Haben, for coming
on the podcast.

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:01.920
