00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:09.820
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:11.370
Well, Mark, question
of the week?

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:12.079
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:18:12.079 --> 00:18:14.369
MELANIE WARRICK: How do I
perform large-scale mutations

00:18:14.369 --> 00:18:14.940
in BigQuery?

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:16.090
MARK MANDEL: This is an
interesting question.

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:18.090
I don't actually work personally
with BigQuery that often.

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:20.690
So I learned a whole bunch of
stuff looking at this today.

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:23.315
There is a blog post that talks
all about it, strangely enough,

00:18:23.315 --> 00:18:25.860
which is why I ended up learning
about it, which is lovely.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:28.417
So BigQuery, it's
our big data query

00:18:28.417 --> 00:18:30.750
system where you can do massive
amounts of data querying

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:33.542
over very large data sets,
terabytes, petabytes of data.

00:18:33.542 --> 00:18:35.250
But sometimes you want
to edit that data.

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:36.244
Maybe it went in wrong.

00:18:36.244 --> 00:18:37.410
Maybe you want to change it.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.660
Maybe you want to delete some
for archive purposes, all

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:40.690
that kind of good stuff.

00:18:40.690 --> 00:18:43.380
So BigQuery does have a
data manipulation language

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.270
that you can use, so
like select statements,

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:46.840
merge statements,
update statements,

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:47.770
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:49.186
But the limit that
you will hit is

00:18:49.186 --> 00:18:51.570
that there are quotas about
the frequency of mutation

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.850
that you can make over
the tables that you have.

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:56.370
This is basically because
we do store lots and lots

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:57.120
and lots of data.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:59.080
And we do very special
things to do it essentially.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:00.720
So maximum number
of combined update,

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:03.367
delete, and merge statements
per day per table is about 200.

00:19:03.367 --> 00:19:04.950
The maximum number
of combined update,

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:06.960
delete, and merge statements
per day per project

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:08.100
is about 10,000.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:09.647
You can definitely make updates.

00:19:09.647 --> 00:19:11.730
And there's no limit of
the amount of size of data

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:13.900
that you manipulate when
you do those manipulations.

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:16.020
So like, a common
scenario might be

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:18.210
where you might want to
update existing data based

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:21.180
on new information coming in
through on a periodic basis.

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:23.290
Just be aware of those
sort of limitations.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:24.664
So maybe you want
to do that sort

00:19:24.664 --> 00:19:26.720
of once a day over the
entire data set if you can

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:28.580
rather than doing it
multiple times per day.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:30.080
Just keep those
limitations in mind.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:32.100
But we'll link to
the article, which

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.440
has other links to other
articles around like what

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:36.820
the quotas are and like what's
available and what's not.

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:38.880
But yeah, as long as you
keep in mind those quotas,

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:39.840
you should be good to go.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:40.410
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:42.270
Everything that's going
on with data in general,

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.340
this is a key thing to
be able to work through.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:45.399
Check out that link.

